trimmed = """We often trade real love for cheap illusions when routine makes us forget the blessings we already have. Deep inside, we all carry a strong desire to feel safe, known, and truly valued. Yet, in our hurry, we look for quick relief in shiny things that promise everything and deliver nothing.

Pull up a chair and share a warm cup of coffee with me. This quiet ache in our chest is not new to our busy world. Many centuries ago, a gentle prophet named Jeremiah spoke about this heartbreak in the Bible. He compared our relationship with the Lord to a marriage that slowly grew cold over time until it was almost torn apart.

### Short memories slowly cool down warm love

Every painful split begins long before an open argument breaks out. It usually starts when our memory begins to fail us. Jeremiah reminded the people that, in the beginning, they walked closely with God like young newlyweds. They walked through rough deserts together, trusting His voice, finding daily peace in His presence, and resting under His faithful shield.

God watched over every tiny step, shielded them from danger, and carried them through dry valleys like a loyal friend. But as life grew comfortable and easy, the people slowly forgot the road behind them. They stopped asking where God was, even though He broke their heavy chains and gave them good water in a parched desert.

When we forget the quiet care we receive each day, our hearts easily turn proud and ungrateful. We begin to think that every good gift came from our own strength or that other people simply owe us their service. The moment gratitude leaves a home, love walks out right behind it.

The exact same tragedy happens around our own kitchen tables every week. We stop noticing the fresh coffee poured with love early in the morning. We overlook the warm hug waiting at the front door after work, and we tune out the kind words spoken when we feel down. Unspoken gratitude builds a high wall of chilly silence between people who care for each other.

### The painful trick of broken buckets

When we turn away from true love, our soul begins to starve. A hungry person will reach for almost any food that looks pretty, even if it is completely spoiled inside. Jeremiah used a picture that a child could understand. He said people turned away from a bubbling fountain of clean spring water just to dig cracked dirt cisterns that cannot hold a drop.

Picture a tired traveler in the hot midday sun, dying of deep thirst. Instead of drinking freely from a clear, running stream right in front of him, he grabs an old bucket full of cracks. He scoops up water, but it leaks through the holes before touching his parched lips. It leaves him standing in the hot dirt, dry, frustrated, and still thirsty.

That is what we do when we chase quick thrills, endless likes on our phones, impulse shopping, or secret romances outside our vows. We keep hoping that one more purchase, one more compliment, or one more adventure will fill the empty space in our chest. Yet thirty minutes later, the quiet sadness returns because a leaky well can never hold life-giving water.

Jesus once met a tired woman beside an old village well in the Gospel of John. She had lived through five failed marriages and was living with another man, carrying a heavy load of loneliness. Jesus spoke to her with tender patience. He showed her that water from earthly wells leaves us thirsty again, but His grace is a living fountain that bubbles up inside and never runs dry.

### The cold silence of slow drifting

No relationship on earth can survive long seasons of cold indifference. In the ancient book of Jeremiah, the emotional distance grew so deep and painful that God used the serious word "divorce." It is a heavy word that makes the heart ache. It reminds us that our Creator is not a cold cosmic force, but a loving Father who feels real sorrow when His children trade His sweet home for worthless junk that leaves them bruised and bleeding.

We often fool ourselves into thinking that unfaithfulness happens in a single split second. In reality, wandering begins long before anyone takes a physical step away. It starts quietly in the secret corners of the mind when we neglect our own garden and daydream about our neighbor's green grass. We start laughing at cheap jokes from strangers while growing annoyed by the sweet loyalty of home.

Before we even realize what is happening, a deep canyon opens up between us and those who truly love us. We may still keep up appearances on Sunday mornings, wear clean clothes, and smile politely for everyone. But inside, our thoughts are miles away, flirting with empty things that cannot save us. That distance leaves our lives chilly, fragile, and completely worn out.

### A fresh start is always waiting for you

The beautiful hope at the heart of this ancient story is that God never throws us away like a paper cup. Even after all our bad trades, foolish choices, and loyalty to leaky buckets, He does not slam the door shut. His love is patient, rooted deep in mercy, and always calls us home with a calm, gentle voice that brings quiet tears of relief.

You do not have to spend another day begging for crumbs in places that only leave you with guilt, regret, and a heavy spirit. A safe harbor is open right now, and a fresh spring of living water is flowing freely for you. You only need to drop your broken buckets, confess that you got lost along the way, and run back into the open arms of the One who never forgot your name.

### A practical step for today

Today, take five quiet minutes alone to sit and look honestly into your own heart. Grab a piece of paper and write down three simple, precious gifts that someone who loves you—or God Himself—has given you that you forgot to acknowledge. Once you write them down, send a short text message to thank that person, or bow your head and pray: "Lord, thank You for watching over me every single day; today I choose to cherish what is real and let go of what is hollow."
"""

words = trimmed.split()
print("Word count:", len(words))
